/*!
    \page tutorial-qml-bridge.html
    \title Tutorial 6: Connecting the Core to QML
    \previouspage {Tutorial 5: A Game Core Like an MMORPG Server}
    \nextpage {Tutorial 7: The Map, Sprites and Oblique Projection}

    The core runs in its own thread and knows no Qt. QML runs in the GUI thread. A thin
    bridge connects them.

    \section1 Events out

    The core reports changes through a plain C++ interface:

    \quotefromfile backend/game_observer.h
    \skipto class igame_observer_t
    \printuntil virtual void on_creature_created

    \section1 Changing threads

    \l GameObserver implements it and moves each event to the GUI thread:

    \quotefromfile backend/GameObserver.cpp
    \skipto void GameObserver::on_creature_health_changed(
    \printuntil Qt::QueuedConnection
    \printuntil }

    \c Qt::QueuedConnection runs the lambda in the GUI thread, so QML never sees half
    updated data.

    \section1 Models and commands

    \l GameBackend owns one list model per kind of object (creatures, bases, base actions,
    corpses) and forwards input to the core:

    \quotefromfile backend/GameBackend.h
    \skipto Q_INVOKABLE void orderBaseAction
    \printline orderBaseAction

    Example use in QML:

    \code
    Repeater {
        model: gameBackend.creaturesModel
        delegate: CreatureView {}
    }

    onClicked: gameBackend.orderBaseAction("spawn:orc")
    \endcode

    The core checks every command. QML cannot, for example, move the units of another race.

    \section1 Adding new data to the screen

    Always the same path:

    \list 1
        \li Add a function to \l igame_observer_t and call it in the core.
        \li Emit a signal from \l GameObserver.
        \li Update a model or a property in \l GameBackend.
        \li Bind to it in QML.
    \endlist
*/
